    "result_text": "All tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification required rejecting a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with HTTP 400.\n\nI added the check in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, alongside the other supplied-field validations (address, telephone, email, postcode), which is where request-level 400 responses already live:\n\n```java\nLocalDate suppliedRegistrationDate = owner.getRegistrationDate();\nif (suppliedRegistrationDate != null && suppliedRegistrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);\n}\nLocalDate registrationDate = BusinessDay.rollForward(\n    suppliedRegistrationDate == null ? LocalDate.now() : suppliedRegistrationDate);\nowner.setRegistrationDate(registrationDate);\n```\n\nKey design points:\n- The check only fires when a date was **supplied** (`!= null`) \u2014 an omitted date still defaults to the server date as before.\n- It validates the **raw supplied** value *before* the business-day roll-forward, so a future date can't slip through by being normalized.\n- It reuses the value already read (`suppliedRegistrationDate`) rather than duplicating the `getRegistrationDate()` calls, and keeps the logic in the controller where all other input-validation 400s already are \u2014 no new responsibility pushed into the `Owner` entity or service layer.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsFutureDate` (posting `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"`) passes, and the full suite is green.",
